# Line endings are normalised to LF *in the repository*. The project is developed on
# Windows and built in CI on Linux; a CRLF that reaches the index turns a shell script
# into a file Linux cannot execute and makes every diff noisy. Working copies still get
# native endings on Windows, so this costs the Windows developer nothing.
* text=auto

# Shell scripts must be LF even in the working tree: CI invokes them on Linux, and a
# stray CR after the shebang is a "bad interpreter" error that reads like a missing file.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary or already-compressed content git must not touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.whl binary
*.uf2 binary
*.bin binary
# Fusion 360 archive. It is a zip using a compression method standard tools do not open,
# so git cannot sniff it reliably -- and normalising line endings inside a compressed
# archive corrupts it silently. Say it outright rather than let the heuristic guess.
*.f3d binary
*.f3z binary

# STEP is ASCII and diffs usefully, so it is normalised like any other text file. It is
# also the format that outlives whichever CAD package produced it, which is why
# mechanical/CAD/README.md asks for one beside every native file.
*.step text
*.stp text
